{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/16/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f14c03\" lines=\"75-83\">\n<description>\nCrop the foreground object whose missing cells match marker shapes, and fill those missing cells with the marker colors.\n\nInput structure\nA dominant background surrounds several monochromatic foreground objects. Exactly one object has background gaps inside its bounding box whose component shapes are matched by separate marker objects.\nRule\nFind that object, crop its bounding box, and replace each background gap component with the color of the separate marker component or components having the same normalized shape.\nKey insight\nThe marker objects do not mark absolute positions; their shapes identify which gaps in the selected object should be colored, while objects with unmatched gaps are distractors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fbf15a0b\" lines=\"49-69\">\n<description>\nCrop out the grid half containing the two aligned marker pixels, then erase the markers to background.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one non-background marker color\n  that appears as exactly two cells.\n- The marker cells are aligned either in one row or one column and both lie\n  in the same half along that direction.\n- Other non-background pixels are content inside one or both halves.\n\nRule\n- If the markers share a row, keep the left or right half whose columns\n  contain them.\n- If the markers share a column, keep the top or bottom half whose rows\n  contain them.\n- In the kept half, recolor the marker pixels to the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two marker pixels are not part of the picture; their alignment and\n  half-position identify which half of the doubled canvas is the answer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\" lines=\"17-34\">\n<description>\nSplit the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"fc7cae8d\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nAlign a central rectangle to two colored frame-edge controls via rotation/reflection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with two solid-colored border edges (distinct non-bg colors).\n- A central filled rectangle with 1px colored guide edges on two sides, colors matching the frame controls.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate/mirror the object so its guide edges face the same sides as the matching frame controls.\n- Crop off the 1px guide edges from aligned sides.\n\nKey insight: Guide-edge colors encode target frame sides; the correct dihedral transform matches both simultaneously.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ecac7f7\" lines=\"23-36\">\n<description>\nCombine the left, middle, and right ordered objects from three separated panels.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is three HxH panels separated by one-cell vertical separator bars.\n- The background is the dominant color, and each panel contains exactly three same-color 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\n- Build a new HxH background canvas.\n- From the left panel copy its leftmost object, from the middle panel copy its middle object, and from the right panel copy its rightmost object.\n\nKey insight\n- The separator bars create three aligned layers, and the panel's own left-to-right object order selects which object survives in the merged output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c920a713\" lines=\"87-102\">\n<description>\nUntangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.\n- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.\n- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.\n\nRule\n- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.\n- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a47bf94d\" lines=\"178-189\">\n<description>\nTransmit colored tokens along wire networks, converting each to its complementary 3×3 pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Non-bg cells form wire networks and 3×3 tokens (solid blocks or X/diagonal patterns) near wire endpoints.\n\nRule:\n- Every solid 3×3 token is replaced in-place by a diamond (center + 4 cardinal neighbors).\n- Each token near a wire endpoint sends its complement to the opposite endpoint: solid emits X, X emits diamond.\n\nKey insight: Token shape cycles solid → X → diamond; wires route each complement to the far end.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\n\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"14b8e18c\" lines=\"40-57\">\n<description>\nMark every complete square object with red pixels just outside its corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform background contains one or more monochrome 4-connected foreground objects.\n- The relevant objects are square frames or filled squares: their bbox is square\n  and all cells on the bbox border have the object's color.\n- Non-square bars and incomplete fragments may also appear and are ignored.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For each complete square object, paint red on the two outside background\n  pixels orthogonally adjacent to each of the square's four corners.\n\nKey insight: The transformation is not based on size or color; it selects\nobjects whose bounding box is a complete square border and labels only their\nexposed outer corner-adjacent cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"48634b99\" lines=\"14-27\">\n<description>\nAdvance the marker from one half of a vertical bar to the same half of the next longer bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms many vertical one-cell-wide bars; the other appears as a single vertical marker segment.\n- The marker segment occupies exactly the top or bottom half of its containing vertical bar.\nRule\n- Recolor the current marker segment back to the bar color.\n- Find the unique all-bar-color vertical bar whose height is two pixels greater than the marked source bar.\n- Recolor the same half of that next longer bar to the marker color.\nKey insight\n- Each bar's total height encodes an ordered size, and the marker advances from a 2k bar to the corresponding 2(k+1) bar while preserving whether it marks the top or bottom half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
